1)  Question :

Imagine you have an N × N chessboard.

You need to place N queens such that:

No two queens are in the same row.
No two queens are in the same column.
No two queens are on the same diagonal.

Return all possible valid board configurations



2)  Simple intuition

Imagine you're filling an exam hall.

There are N rows.

Each row must have exactly one student.

Before seating a student in a seat, you check

"Will this student cheat with anyone already seated?"

If yes

➡️ Try another seat.

If no

➡️ Seat the student and move to the next row.

If later no seat works,

➡️ Remove the previously seated student and try another seat.

That is exactly backtracking.




3)  Logic :

solved this problem using the backtracking approach.
Since each row must contain exactly one queen,  process the board row by row. For every row,  try placing the queen in each column one by one.
Before placing a queen,  check whether the current position is safe by ensuring that no previously placed queen is present in the same column, the upper-left diagonal, or the upper-right diagonal.
*Since queens are placed row by row from top to bottom, I only need to check the rows above the current row.

If the position is safe,  place the queen and recursively move to the next row.
If it successfully place queens in all rows,  convert the board into the required string representation and store it as one valid solution.
If at any point no valid position exists in the current row,  backtrack by removing the previously placed queen and trying the next available column.
This process continues until every possible valid arrangement has been explored


4)  Code :

class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        boolean[][] queen = new boolean[n][n];
        return nqueen(queen,0);
        
    }
      static List<List<String>> nqueen(boolean[][] queen,int row){
        List<List<String>> list = new ArrayList<>();
        if(row==queen.length){
           list.add(display(queen));
           return list;
        }
        for (int coln = 0; coln < queen.length; coln++) {
            if(safe(queen,row,coln)){
                queen[row][coln]=true;
                list.addAll(nqueen(queen,row+1));
                queen[row][coln]=false;
            }
        }
        return list;

    }
        static List<String> display(boolean[][] queen) {
            List<String> list=new ArrayList<>();
        for(boolean[] maze:queen){
            String str="";
            for(boolean element:maze){
                if(element){
                   str+="Q";
                }
                else{
                    str+=".";
                }
            }
            list.add(str);
        }
        return list;
    }
     static boolean safe(boolean[][] queen, int row, int coln) {
        //vertical check
        for (int i = 0; i < row; i++) {
            if(queen[i][coln]){
                return false;
            }
        }
        // left diagonal
        int maxleft=Math.min(row,coln);
        for (int j = 1; j <= maxleft; j++) {
            if(queen[row-j][coln-j]){
                return false;
            }
        }
        // left diagonal
        int maxright=Math.min(row,queen.length-coln-1);
        for (int j = 1; j <= maxright; j++) {
            if(queen[row-j][coln+j]){
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;

    }

}
